<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Copperbelt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Copperbelt is a region of rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> deposits straddling the border of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. It has been mined since early times. Trade in its copper linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a> to wider regional networks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copperbelt.jpg" alt="Copperbelt" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taylor, C.D., Schulz, K.J., Doebrich, J.L., Orris, G.J., Denning, P.D., and Kirschbaum, M.J. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_African_Copper_Belt.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The region lies on the border between central-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, though in some contexts the term excludes the Congo entirely. It takes in the mining towns of Zambia's Copperbelt Province, among them Ndola, Kitwe, Chingola, Luanshya and Mufulira, and those of the Congo's Haut-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lualaba">Lualaba</a> provinces, such as Lubumbashi, Kolwezi and Likasi. The ores lie in the Katanga Supergroup, a Neoproterozoic sequence of formations with rich stratiform <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-cobalt deposits; chalcopyrite, bornite and chalcocite occur in the Lower Roan Formation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Since the Bantu expansion both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>'s Copperbelt have been called Ilamba, or Lambaland, after the Lamba people, who came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> kingdoms, settled at Lake Kashiba and spread outward from there. In 1895 the American scout Frederick Russell Burnham led the expedition of the Northern Territories (BSA) Exploration Co. that established the presence of major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> deposits. Along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kafue-river">Kafue River</a> he met people wearing copper bracelets, and he reported that the natives had worked the ore for ages, as their old dumps showed, and traded their handiwork as far as the Portuguese of the west coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> of the east.</p><p>Years later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> built towns along the river and a railway to carry the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. During the 1950s the Copperbelt was the largest copper-producing area in the world, with the Roan Antelope, Nkana, Nchanga, Mufulira and Rokana mines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>'s part became a province soon after independence in 1964 under the name Western Province, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda">Kenneth Kaunda</a> renamed it Copperbelt Province in 1969. Its mines include Chambishi, Kansanshi, Lumwana and Konkola, and those in the Congo include Kamoto, Kipushi, Kolwezi and Ruashi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Copperbelt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade linked the heartland with the <b>Copperbelt</b> to the south, exchanging copper crosses and other copper ingots, dried fish, salt and iron tools for ivory, slaves and luxury goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/luba-empire">Luba Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade networks reached the <b>Copperbelt</b> and neighbouring polities, linking Lunda markets with the Luba, Luvale (Lwena) and other Central African peoples, and westwards through the Imbangala kingdom of Kasanje to Portuguese Angola.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Copperbelt</b> strikes brought the heaviest violence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late 1920s the <b>Copperbelt</b> grew into one of the world's leading copper producers, drawing African workers whose strikes in 1935 and 1940 were early signs of organised protest.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
